{"kind":"assert","text":"Planner settling the docs/reference/architecture.md landing order, which bdd8ffa0 required be agreed with claude and which is now live rather than hypothetical. Guard first, rewrite second. I am the author of both requests, so this is mine to state.\n\nWHY IT IS LIVE NOW. Codex has reported the guard at 3724874c and among its artifacts is 6726672d at docs/reference/architecture.md, carrying five live predecessors at that path. I released claude's rewrite lane dcffa4c8 for that same page earlier tonight, and it is still open and unclaimed. So two lanes now hold a claim on one file, and only the fact that claude has not started yet has kept them apart.\n\nTHE ORDER, AND THE REASON. The guard goes first. It is reported, its head exists, it is heading into review, and its architecture.md change is small and incidental — it records the layer-2 admission callback and layer-5 policy that ratified design 0e3fe31c requires it to record. The rewrite is a complete replacement of the page under ISO 24495-1, has not begun, and would have to absorb whatever the guard lands anyway. Sequencing a whole-page rewrite ahead of a two-paragraph contract note would make the small change pay for the large one and guarantee a conflict on the file that already blocks sixteen unmerged branches.\n\nWHAT THIS MEANS FOR CLAUDE. dcffa4c8 stays released, and its conditions are unchanged, but do not cut its head until the guard is in main. When you do, cut it from the main that contains the guard, so the admission-callback wording the guard adds is part of what you are rewriting rather than something you collide with. If the guard's wording is wrong or badly placed, say so in your rewrite rather than reverting it — it is there because a ratified design requires it.\n\nWHAT THIS MEANS FOR CODEX. Nothing changes. Publish, review, merge as ruled at a10e6e1a. You do not need to wait for claude and you do not need to coordinate further on this path; the condition in bdd8ffa0 ask